  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>CENTCOM conducted the most extensive strike package since the MOU collapse on July 15–16, with B-2 Spirit sorties targeting missile production facilities in Iran's missile province and the Fars and Isfahan districts, and expanding the strike envelope to within 30km of Tehran for the first time since the war began. Iran's civil aviation authority issued emergency airspace closures over a 200km radius around Tehran, confirmed by FlightRadar24 data as of 06:00 UTC on July 16. A US Navy guided-missile destroyer struck three IRGC naval patrol vessels in the Strait of Hormuz on July 15, destroying two. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The IRGC ballistic missile command struck the Al-Tanf garrison in Syria and a US forward operating base in Jordan on July 15; the Pentagon confirmed 7 US service members wounded and no fatalities. Iran's Foreign Ministry spokesperson Baghaei stated at a July 16 press briefing that Iran has 'no plans for negotiations under military coercion' and that the Islamabad MOU 'is no longer a viable framework' — a position carried by all Iranian state-media outlets within hours of the briefing. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Qatar's Foreign Ministry confirmed on July 14 that shuttle diplomacy continued via Qatari and Pakistani channels, with Pakistan's National Security Adviser in Doha through July 13–14. Trump posted on Truth Social on July 15 that Iran 'wants to meet and talk' but would face 'much bigger strikes next week' targeting power generation and bridge infrastructure unless contact was established within 72 hours. The IAEA's July 11 report confirmed Iran's uranium enrichment held at 60%, with no enrichment to weapons-grade material detected. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>UANI's Strait of Hormuz vessel tracker recorded approximately 10 commercial transits per day as a 7-day average for July 9–15, down from a peak of approximately 52 on June 24–25 and near the war-low recorded at initial closure. UK-French coalition minehunting vessels (RFA Lyme Bay as mother ship, two French minehunters) remained positioned in Omani waters with operations suspended following the July 6–7 vessel strike incidents; UK Royal Navy sources cited unresolved rules-of-engagement protocols for hostile-fire environments. P&amp;I war-risk insurance ran at approximately 8× pre-war levels, with 6 major P&amp;I clubs having withdrawn hull cover for Hormuz transits. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset legal adviser Sagit Afik confirmed the dissolution date as July 17, with the October 27, 2026 election date formally established under Israeli law. The most recent Haaretz poll (July 9) showed the opposition bloc projected at 69 seats versus the coalition's 51 — the largest opposition lead in published polling since the Gaza war began. Netanyahu's caretaker government retains executive authority through dissolution with no expected change of government before the October 27 election. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

