  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Press TV opened their July 16 editions with coverage of what Tehran described as a 'second war of aggression against the Islamic Republic,' carrying IRGC General Salami's statement that 'the enemy will receive a response ten times greater for every blow.' State media published no acknowledgment of CENTCOM strike damage to missile facilities; coverage was limited to the IRGC's retaliatory strikes on US bases in Jordan and Syria, described as 'precision defensive operations conducted within the bounds of international law.' → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Kayhan International ran a front-page editorial characterizing the Strait of Hormuz closure as a 'sovereign right under international law,' describing coalition MCM vessels as a 'foreign military invasion of Persian territorial waters' warranting further Iranian naval action. Tasnim News Agency carried no coverage of Foreign Minister Baghaei's statement as a reversal of prior policy; it was framed as an affirmation of 'principled sovereignty against coercive military pressure.' The Hormuz closure narrative in domestic media held the closure as a strategic asset, not a liability. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Khamenei's reported injuries from an alleged Israeli drone strike near his Tehran residence remained unconfirmed by Iranian state sources as of July 16; IRNA issued a brief denial describing normal Supreme Leader activities, with Khamenei's office releasing a photograph of a meeting with IRGC commanders dated July 15. The $6 billion in frozen assets from the 2023 Qatar agreement was the central grievance in domestic messaging: Kayhan and IRNA both framed US refusal to release these assets as evidence of 'American bad faith in any future negotiations.' → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

